## Why This Is Asked

This question evaluates the ability to design a runtime-extensible command processing system while balancing dynamic plugin loading with reliability and isolation. It surfaces trade-offs between discoverability, versioning, and failure handling.

## Key Concepts

- **Command pattern** with a pluggable registry for runtime flexibility
- **Runtime registration** of handlers enabling plugin-based architecture
- **Idempotency guarantees** to prevent duplicate processing
- **Isolated execution** to ensure fault containment between commands
- **Retry policy** with exponential backoff for transient failures
- **Testing implications** for dynamic behavior and handler contracts

## Code Example

```typescript
interface Command {
  name: string;
  payload: unknown;
  idempotencyKey?: string;
}

interface CommandHandler<T = unknown> {
  handles: string;
  execute(payload: T): Promise<void>;
}

class CommandDispatcher {
  private handlers = new Map<string, CommandHandler>();

  register(handler: CommandHandler): void {
    this.handlers.set(handler.handles, handler);
  }

  async dispatch(command: Command): Promise<void> {
    const handler = this.handlers.get(command.name);
    if (!handler) throw new Error(`Unknown command: ${command.name}`);
    await handler.execute(command.payload);
  }
}
```

## Trade-offs

| Concern | Trade-off |
|---------|-----------|
| Runtime flexibility | Adds complexity; handlers must implement strict contracts |
| Isolation | Worker processes increase memory overhead |
| Idempotency | Requires persistent storage; may limit throughput |
| Discovery | Central registry can become a bottleneck at scale |

## Why This Is Asked

